Here's a complete example of a C# MCP server that wraps a REST API:

---

## Tool Class (`WeatherTools.cs`)

```csharp
using ModelContextProtocol.Server;
using System.ComponentModel;
using System.Text.Json;

[McpServerToolType]
public class WeatherTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<WeatherTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("Get current weather for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetWeather(
        [Description("City name (e.g. 'London')")] string city,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching weather for {City}", city);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/v1/weather?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }

    [McpServerTool, Description("Get 5-day forecast for a city.")]
    public async Task<string> GetForecast(
        [Description("City name")] string city,
        [Description("Number of days (1-5)")] int days = 3,
        CancellationToken cancellationToken = default)
    {
        logger.LogInformation("Fetching {Days}-day forecast for {City}", days, city);
        var response = await httpClient.GetStringAsync(
            $"/v1/forecast?q={Uri.EscapeDataString(city)}&days={days}",
            cancellationToken);
        return response;
    }
}
```

## Program.cs (stdio transport)

```csharp
using Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Hosting;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;

var builder = Host.CreateApplicationBuilder(args);

// CRITICAL: Log to stderr only — stdout is the JSON-RPC transport
builder.Logging.AddConsole(options =>
    options.LogToStandardErrorThreshold = LogLevel.Trace);

// Register HttpClient with a base address for the API
builder.Services.AddHttpClient<WeatherTools>(client =>
{
    client.BaseAddress = new Uri("https://api.weatherapi.com");
});

builder.Services.AddMcpServer()
    .WithStdioServerTransport()
    .WithToolsFromAssembly();

await builder.Build().RunAsync();
```

---

## How DI Works for MCP Tools

The SDK supports **two injection styles**:

### 1. Constructor injection (non-static class) — preferred for services

```csharp
[McpServerToolType]
public class MyTools(HttpClient httpClient, ILogger<MyTools> logger)
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("...")]
    public async Task<string> DoWork(...) { /* use httpClient */ }
}
```

The class is resolved from the DI container each time a tool is called. Register dependencies normally in `Program.cs` (`AddHttpClient`, `AddSingleton`, etc.).

### 2. Method parameter injection (static class) — simpler one-off tools

```csharp
[McpServerToolType]
public static class SimpleTools
{
    [McpServerTool, Description("...")]
    public static async Task<string> DoWork(
        [Description("input")] string input,
        HttpClient httpClient,          // injected, NOT in schema
        CancellationToken ct = default) { ... }
}
```

**Key rule:** Parameters that are DI-registered services, `CancellationToken`, `IMcpServer`, or `IProgress<T>` are **automatically injected** and never appear in the tool's JSON schema exposed to the LLM. Only parameters with `[Description]` are visible to the model.

### What gets exposed to the LLM vs. injected:

| Parameter type | Appears in schema? | Source |
|---|---|---|
| `[Description("...")]` annotated | ✅ Yes | LLM provides value |
| `CancellationToken` | ❌ No | Framework injects |
| `ILogger<T>`, `HttpClient`, etc. | ❌ No | DI container injects |
| `IMcpServer` | ❌ No | Framework injects |
| `IProgress<ProgressNotificationValue>` | ❌ No | Framework injects |

This design keeps your tool signatures clean while giving full access to infrastructure services.
